---
eleventyImport:
  collections: ["post"]
---
{%- comment %} Task 20 review fix (Finding 2): this layout is the root of
every page's layout chain, so declaring the collections.post dependency here
once — rather than on every individual content file — covers all of them;
matches the same eleventyImport convention content/opengraph-images.liquid
already uses for its own collections.post read (test/build.mjs, "templates
reading collections declare eleventyImport where it can take effect").

Deliberately collections.post, not collections.all: only post pages have a
`selfEntry` to look up below (homepage/about/search fall straight through to
site.description, no lookup needed). collections.all also transitively pulls
in @11ty/eleventy-plugin-rss's virtual feed template — which itself declares
`eleventyImport: { collections: ["post"] }` and is otherwise excluded only
from collections.post, not collections.all (node_modules/@11ty/eleventy-
plugin-rss/src/virtualTemplate.js) — creating a real dependency-graph
interaction: proven empirically (25 warm-up Eleventy() instantiations in one
process, then a build+serve pair, matching test/build.mjs's "OG card page
set is gated..." check) to drop one post's OG card from the paginated set.
collections.post alone does not reproduce it, and is exactly what
content/opengraph-images.liquid already depends on without issue. {% endcomment -%}
{%- capture fullUrl %}{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}{% endcapture -%}
{%- comment %} Task 20 review fix (Finding 2): "A post by " was a literal
string — site.author.name is empty by design (theme is neutral). Compute a
real description per spec §F.7's chain: explicit front matter `description`,
else this page's own body text (plain, truncated), else the site's own
description — never empty, never the old placeholder.

`content` here is NOT this page's own body: base.liquid is the outermost
layout, so by this point `content` already holds default.liquid's full
rendered output (skip link + nav rail + menu) with the real page body buried
inside it (see default.liquid, entry.liquid, page.liquid). Piping that
through ogPlain would put "Skip to Content <site name> Home About Archives
Projects Speaking Uses Domains Menu" ahead of the real text on every page.
Look this page up in collections.post instead (see the comment on
eleventyImport above for why collections.post and not collections.all) and
read `.templateContent` — Eleventy's pre-layout render of just this page's
own body, the same property content/opengraph-images.liquid's own partial
already reads via `entry.templateContent` (see that file's comment),
resolved here by URL instead of being handed a pagination alias directly.
Safe to read here: collections (including templateContent) are fully
resolved before any individual page is rendered/written — the same reason
the OG card partial already does this without a circular-reference error.
A non-post page (homepage, search, about) has no entry in collections.post,
so `selfEntry` is simply undefined there and the chain falls straight
through to site.description below — not an error case. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign selfEntry = collections.post | where: "url", page.url | first -%}
{%- assign ogDesc = description | default: selfEntry.templateContent | ogPlain | ogTruncate: 200 | default: site.description -%}
{%- comment %} Finding 12: the site name was appended unconditionally, so any
   page whose own title IS the site name (the homepage, content/index.liquid)
   rendered "Indiekit reference theme—Indiekit reference theme" — and the same
   doubling reached og:title and twitter:title, i.e. every link preview.
   Composed once here rather than repeating the conditional in three tags.
   Two variables, not one, because <title> honours titleOverride and the
   OG/Twitter tags deliberately do not — preserved as-is. {% endcomment -%}
{%- assign docTitleText = titleOverride | default: title -%}
{%- if docTitleText and docTitleText != site.name -%}
{%- capture docTitle %}{{ docTitleText }}—{{ site.name }}{% endcapture -%}
{%- else -%}{%- assign docTitle = site.name -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- if title and title != site.name -%}
{%- capture ogTitle %}{{ title }}—{{ site.name }}{% endcapture -%}
{%- else -%}{%- assign ogTitle = site.name -%}{%- endif -%}
<!doctype html>
{%- assign headingFontState = "custom" -%}
{%- if site.theme.fonts.heading == "Hex Franklin Tight" -%}{%- assign headingFontState = "default" -%}{%- endif -%}
<html lang="en" data-scheme="{{ site.theme.scheme }}" data-scheme-default="{{ site.theme.scheme }}" data-rail="{{ site.theme.rail.side }}" data-heading-font="{{ headingFontState }}"{% if tmpl or templateclass or classes.html or site.theme.signature.spectrum %} class="{% if tmpl %}tmpl-{{ tmpl }}{% endif %}{% if templateclass %} {{ templateclass }}{% endif %}{%- if classes.html %} {{ classes.html }}{% endif -%}{%- if site.theme.signature.spectrum %} page-gradient{% endif -%}"{% endif %}{% if eleventy.env.runMode == "serve" %} data-eleventy-runmode="{{ eleventy.env.runMode }}"{% endif %}>
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8">
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

		<title>{{ docTitle }}</title>

		{%- comment %} Webmention discovery. Without these a sender cannot find
		   where to deliver, so the site can receive nothing — the receiving half
		   of webmentions is these two lines, not the comment list.
		   The source hardcodes its own webmention.io endpoint; this theme derives
		   it from site.json's url via the same hostnameFromUrl filter
		   webmentionlist.liquid's send-form already uses. {% endcomment -%}
		{%- if site.url %}
		<link rel="webmention" href="https://webmention.io/{{ site.url | hostnameFromUrl }}/webmention">
		<link rel="pingback" href="https://webmention.io/{{ site.url | hostnameFromUrl }}/xmlrpc">
		{%- endif %}
		<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/lato/2.0/latolatin-regular-subset.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
		<link rel="preload" href="/fonts/hex-franklin/HEX_Franklin_v0.2_Tyght_Variable-subset.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>

{%- if site.author.name %}
		<meta name="author" content="{{ site.author.name }}">
{%- endif %}
{%- comment %} Mastodon and other fediverse servers read this to attribute
	   a link preview to its author. Source emits it (base.liquid:34); this
	   port dropped it. Sourced from site.json rather than hardcoded. {% endcomment -%}
{%- if site.author.fediverse %}
		<meta name="fediverse:creator" content="{{ site.author.fediverse }}">
{%- endif %}
		<meta name="description" content="{{ ogDesc | escape }}">
		<meta name="generator" content="{{ eleventy.generator }}">

{%- if noindex %}
		{% comment %} General-purpose noindex flag. Task 20's own OG card
		pages (content/opengraph-images.liquid) don't route through this
		layout at all — see _includes/layouts/opengraph.liquid, which sets
		its own unconditional noindex — so this guard is for any other page
		that sets `noindex: true` in front matter. {% endcomment %}
		<meta name="robots" content="noindex">
{%- endif %}

		{% comment %} TODO(Task 11): a real favicon is a README/asset concern —
		restore the source's <link rel="apple-touch-icon-precomposed">/site
		avatar icon once the icon files exist in this theme.
		<!-- Icons -->
		<link rel="apple-touch-icon-precomposed" href="{% getUrlForImage site.avatarImgSrc, 192, 'png' %}">
		<link rel="icon" href="{% getUrlForImage site.avatarImgSrc, 192, 'jpeg' %}" sizes="192x192" type="image/jpeg">
		{% endcomment %}
		{% comment %} Task (visual fix): with no <link rel="icon"> at all,
		every page 404s on the browser's own default GET /favicon.ico — this
		theme genuinely ships no icon (see TODO above), so an explicit empty
		icon reference is "reference none" made real, not a new asset.
		{% endcomment %}
		<link rel="icon" href="data:,">

		<!-- Social -->
		{% comment %} Task 20. og:image is `ogImageSource` (_11ty/imagePlugin.js)
		— mechanism 1, this site's OWN card/photo, never
		`getOpenGraphImageUrl` (mechanism 2, which reads a THIRD-PARTY
		page's own og:image and is unrelated to this tag — see that
		function's own comment in imagePlugin.js). postType/photo/title are
		already in scope here via the ordinary data cascade, the same way
		title/description above are. Never emits an empty content attribute
		(contrast the port source, imagePlugin.js:219 — see
		content/opengraph-images.liquid's front-matter comment for why that
		isn't ported). {% endcomment %}
{%- comment %} Task 20 review fix (Finding 1): title/description are
Micropub-supplied, i.e. remote input — every interpolation of them (and of
ogAlt, which embeds title too) into an attribute value below is `| escape`d.
Same defect class as _11ty/imageAvatarPlugin.js's escapeAttr fix: an
unescaped value here breaks out of `content="..."` at the first `"` and
leaks raw markup into the tag. og:image/twitter:image are NOT escaped here —
they're URLs built by `absoluteSiteUrl`/`encodeURIComponent` in
_11ty/imagePlugin.js, which already percent-encode `"`/`<`/`>` in the path,
so no raw quote or bracket ever reaches this attribute. ogAlt is captured
once and reused for both og:image:alt and twitter:image:alt — a shortcode
call can't be piped through `| escape` directly (`{% tag %} | filter` isn't
valid Liquid), so it's captured into a variable first, same as `fullUrl`
above. {% endcomment -%}
{%- capture ogAlt %}{% ogImageAlt postType, photo, title %}{% endcapture -%}
		<meta property="og:title" content="{{ ogTitle | escape }}">
		<meta property="og:type" content="{% if postType %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
		<meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ site.name }}">
		<meta property="og:description" content="{{ ogDesc | escape }}">
		<meta property="og:image" content="{% ogImageSource page.url, postType, photo %}">
		<meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
		<meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
		<meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ ogAlt | escape }}">
		<meta property="og:url" content="{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}">
{%- if postType and date %}
		<meta property="article:published_time" content="{{ date | isoInstant }}">
{%- endif %}

		<!-- X / Twitter: og:* covers most of it; these five change the card
		size and are cheap to add (spec §G.11 — source has none at all). -->
		<meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
		<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ ogTitle | escape }}">
		<meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ ogDesc | escape }}">
		<meta name="twitter:image" content="{% ogImageSource page.url, postType, photo %}">
		<meta name="twitter:image:alt" content="{{ ogAlt | escape }}">

		<!-- SEO/Feeds -->
		<link rel="canonical" href="{{ fullUrl }}">
{% if rssFeedUrl and rssFeedTitle %}
		<link rel="alternate" href="{{ rssFeedUrl }}" title="{{ rssFeedTitle }}" type="application/atom+xml">
{% else %}
		<link rel="alternate" href="/feed.xml" title="{{ site.name }} Posts Feed" type="application/atom+xml">
		{%- comment %} application/feed+json is the registered type for JSON Feed;
		   the spec notes many feeds still use the generic application/json and asks
		   readers to accept it, but recommends publishing the specific one. {% endcomment %}
		<link rel="alternate" href="/feed.json" title="{{ site.name }} Posts Feed" type="application/feed+json">
		{%- comment %} The source advertises a second feed at /follow/ (an activity
		   feed aggregating its own posts plus external sources). This theme builds no
		   such page, so the link pointed at a 404 on every page. The Atom feed at
		   /feed.xml is advertised below. {% endcomment -%}
{% endif %}

{%- comment %} A visitor's stored scheme is applied before the first paint, so
   a dark choice never flashes light. Only the two explicit values are honoured;
   anything else leaves the configured default in place. {% endcomment -%}
{%- if site.theme.schemeToggle %}
		<script>(function(){try{var s=localStorage.getItem("scheme");if(s==="light"||s==="dark"){document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-scheme",s);}}catch(e){}})();</script>
{%- endif %}
{% comment %} Critical CSS in production, blocking stylesheet for local dev
for better livereload — livereload can't hot-swap an inlined <style>.
Ported from zachleat.com, _includes/layouts/base.liquid:45-51. The source's
production branch uses `{% renderFile "./static/initial.scss" %}`; this
theme has no `renderFile` shortcode, so `inlineCriticalCss` (sassPlugin.js)
compiles the same file directly with the `sass` dependency the plugin
already imports. {% endcomment %}
{% if eleventy.env.runMode == "serve" %}
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="/_css/initial.css">
{% else %}
		<style>{% inlineCriticalCss %}</style>
{% endif %}
		<style>{% getBundle 'css' %}</style><!-- WebC -->

		<!-- Preconnect to Eleventy services -->
		<link href="https://v1.opengraph.11ty.dev" rel="preconnect" crossorigin>
		<link href="https://v1.indieweb-avatar.11ty.dev" rel="preconnect" crossorigin>

{% comment %} Deferred CSS: non-render-blocking media="print" swap trick,
gated on JS_ENABLED like the source (zachleat.com base.liquid:56-64) —
without JS the onload handler that flips media back to "all" never runs, so
the else branch falls back to a plain blocking link instead of shipping CSS
that would never apply. This theme has no `stylesheets`/`stylesheetsDist`
collections (nothing populates them), so those loops from the source aren't
ported. {% endcomment %}
{%- if JS_ENABLED %}
		<link rel="stylesheet" href="/_css/defer.css" media="print" onload="this.media='all'">
		<noscript>
			<link href="/_css/defer.css" rel="stylesheet">
		</noscript>
{%- else %}
		<link href="/_css/defer.css" rel="stylesheet">
{%- endif %}

{%- if JS_ENABLED %}
		{% comment %} TODO(unresolved shortcode): the source also gated a goatcounter analytics `<script>` on runMode == "build" here — analytics dropped per scope. {% endcomment %}
		{% comment %} Known gap, not a pending task: same as the CSS bundle above —
		the WebC plugin registers the "js" bundle (eleventy.config.js's
		pluginWebc call triggers eleventyConfig.addBundle("js", ...)), so this
		shortcode would resolve, not error. Left commented out anyway: this theme
		now has no .webc components at all — the only one (toggle-button.webc)
		was deleted with finding 13 — so nothing feeds this bundle and wiring it
		would render an empty <script type="module"></script> with no observable
		effect. Wire it together with whichever future component first uses
		`webc:bundle="js"`.
		<script type="module">{% getBundle 'js' %}</script><!-- WebC -->
		{% endcomment %}
		<script src="/static/defer.js" type="module"></script>
{%- endif %}

		<meta property="eleventy:path" content="{{ page.inputPath }}">

		<!-- IndieAuth -->
{%- comment %} RelMeAuth — one of the two IndieAuth mechanisms Indiekit
	   implements — works by cross-linking MULTIPLE identities: the site links
	   to its profiles, each profile links back, and the provider verifies the
	   pair. A single scalar URL cannot express that, so author.me is an array
	   (source emits six rel=me links, base.liquid:83-88). author.url stays as
	   the canonical profile and is emitted first when set. {% endcomment -%}
{%- if site.author.url %}
		<link href="{{ site.author.url }}" rel="me">
{%- endif %}
{%- for meUrl in site.author.me %}
		<link href="{{ meUrl }}" rel="me">
{%- endfor %}
	</head>
	<body class="{%- if classes and classes.body %} {{ classes.body }}{% endif -%}"
		{% unless excludeFromSearch %} data-pagefind-body{% endunless %}>
		{{ content }}
{%- comment %} The speedlify widget lives in _includes/footer.html ONLY. A
		second copy used to sit here, so enabling it rendered TWO
		<speedlify2-score> elements and loaded the 22KB module twice on every
		page. layouts/default.liquid is the only template that uses this layout
		and it always includes footer.html, so this copy could never reach a page
		the footer did not — it was pure duplication. Its gate was also weaker
		(it checked .enabled but not .url, so an enabled-but-unconfigured site
		emitted speedlify-url=""). {% endcomment -%}
{%- if JS_ENABLED %}
{%- for url in scripts -%}
		<script src="{{ url }}" defer></script>
{%- endfor %}
{%- for url in modules -%}
{%- if url == "/heading-anchors.js" and site.theme.signature.anchors == false %}{% continue %}{% endif -%}
		<script src="{{ url }}" type="module"></script>
{%- endfor %}
{%- endif %}
	</body>
</html>